---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import ArchitectureDiagram from "../components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro";
import NetworkDiagram from "../components/NetworkDiagram.astro";

const toc = [
  ["what", "What is an offline library?"],
  ["storage", "How content is stored"],
  ["access", "How local access works"],
  ["sharing", "Sharing over Wi-Fi"],
  ["reader-server", "Reader vs. local server"],
  ["devices", "Supported devices"],
  ["external", "External storage"],
  ["limits", "Limitations"],
  ["backups", "Backups"],
  ["related", "Kiwix & Internet-in-a-Box"],
];
---
<Base title="How It Works" description="How Reinvent the Wheel stores knowledge on a device, serves it locally and shares it over Wi-Fi without the internet.">
  <section class="section page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1>A library that lives on your device.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        RTW keeps knowledge in ordinary files on a device you own, and serves it as web pages — to that device
        and to anyone nearby on the same Wi-Fi. Here's what happens, without the jargon.
      </p>
      <nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page">
        {toc.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <ArchitectureDiagram />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose-grid">
      <article id="what">
        <h2>What is an offline knowledge library?</h2>
        <p>
          It's a collection of useful material — an encyclopedia, books, maps, manuals, notes — copied onto a device so it can be used without any
          connection. You download it once, while you're online. After that, reading it never touches the internet.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article id="storage">
        <h2>How downloaded content is stored</h2>
        <p>
          As plain files in plain folders: one folder per section (Wikipedia, books, notes, maps, downloads, sites). Wikipedia and similar collections come as
          single compressed <strong>ZIM</strong> files from Kiwix; maps as a single <strong>PMTiles</strong> file; books as PDFs or EPUBs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because they're ordinary files, you can add or back them up with any file manager — and nothing is locked inside a proprietary database.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article id="access">
        <h2>How local access works</h2>
        <p>
          The device runs a small web server (<a href="https://nginx.org">nginx</a>) alongside Kiwix's own server. You open the library in a browser at
          <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080</code> — an address that always means "this device". The pages, search and maps all load from local storage.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article id="sharing">
        <h2>How offline Wi-Fi sharing works</h2>
        <p>
          Wi-Fi is a radio network between nearby devices. The internet is something a router <em>may</em> also provide — but it isn't required for
          devices on the same network to talk to each other. When a phone joins the host's hotspot and opens the library's address, the host answers
          directly.
        </p>
        <div class="diagram"><NetworkDiagram compact /></div>
      </article>

      <article id="reader-server">
        <h2>The difference between a reader and a local server</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-2 compare">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="icon-tile"><Icon name="book" /></span>
            <h3>A reader</h3>
            <p>An app that opens offline content on one device, for one person — like the Kiwix app reading a ZIM file.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="icon-tile"><Icon name="server" /></span>
            <h3>A local server</h3>
            <p>One device holds the content and serves it to many. Readers need nothing but a browser. This is what RTW sets up.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="devices">
        <h2>Supported device architectures</h2>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li><Icon name="check" size={16} /><span><strong>macOS</strong> on Apple Silicon or Intel — available from source, tested on Apple Silicon.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="check" size={16} /><span><strong>Android</strong> on 64-bit ARM (arm64-v8a) through Termux — in testing.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="check" size={16} /><span><strong>Readers:</strong> any device with a reasonably modern web browser, including older phones.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article id="external">
        <h2>Optional external storage</h2>
        <p>
          Large collections can outgrow internal storage, so keeping a library on a USB-C drive is a natural idea. Whether it works depends on the device's
          USB-C support, the drive's filesystem and Android's storage permissions — Android in particular restricts where apps may write on removable media.
        </p>
        <p class="note"><strong>Not supported yet.</strong> External storage hasn't been tested on either platform. <a href="/docs/external-usb-storage/">What's known so far.</a></p>
      </article>

      <article id="limits">
        <h2>Common operating limitations</h2>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li><Icon name="dot" size={16} /><span>The host has to stay on and awake while others use it.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="dot" size={16} /><span>Android may stop background apps to save battery — Termux needs "No restrictions".</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="dot" size={16} /><span>Range is limited to the Wi-Fi network, typically a room or a building.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="dot" size={16} /><span>Content is a snapshot: it doesn't update until you download a newer copy.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="dot" size={16} /><span>Browsers only share location with HTTPS pages, so map location on other devices needs a one-time certificate warning.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article id="backups">
        <h2>The importance of backups</h2>
        <p>
          A library on one device is one dropped phone away from gone. Copy your content folder somewhere else — another drive, another computer, a second device.
          Some sources change or disappear over time, so your copy may become the only one you can get. <a href="/docs/backup-and-recovery/">Backup &amp; Recovery</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article id="related">
        <h2>How RTW relates to Kiwix and Internet-in-a-Box</h2>
        <p>
          RTW doesn't replace them — it depends on them and is inspired by them.
        </p>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li><Icon name="check" size={16} /><span><a href="https://kiwix.org">Kiwix</a> creates the ZIM format and the server RTW uses to serve Wikipedia and other collections. RTW runs <code>kiwix-serve</code> unmodified.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="check" size={16} /><span><a href="https://internet-in-a-box.org">Internet-in-a-Box</a> pioneered the "library in a box" idea, typically on a Raspberry Pi with many optional services. RTW is a lighter take aimed at the devices people already own — a Mac or an Android phone, without root.</span></li>
          <li><Icon name="check" size={16} /><span>Maps come from <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> data via <a href="https://protomaps.com">Protomaps</a>, rendered with <a href="https://maplibre.org">MapLibre</a>.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">Each project keeps its own license. Content downloaded through these tools carries the license of its original publisher.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-bottom: 32px; }
  .page-hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.6rem); max-width: 16ch; }
  .page-hero .lead { max-width: 60ch; }
  .jump { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-top: 28px; }
  .jump a { font-size: .82rem; padding: 6px 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 999px; text-decoration: none; color: var(--muted); }
  .jump a:hover { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--border-strong); }
  .prose-grid { display: grid; gap: 56px; max-width: 820px; }
  .prose-grid article h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem); }
  .prose-grid article > p { color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 86%, var(--muted)); }
  .prose-grid a { color: var(--accent); }
  .diagram { border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px; background: var(--surface); margin-top: 20px; }
  .compare { margin-top: 8px; }
  .note a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
